# Node & transport topology — the target model. A "node" is a keyholder that folds
# the converging DAG; a relay is just a node that happens to be permanent.
# Identity resolves by chain-walk to a member root, identically on every node;
# trust is an edge decision and relay admission is a separate, operational gate.
direction: right

core: "core (Go)\nstore · allow-list gate · fold · sync driver\n+ transport seam" {
  style.fill: "#eef2ff"; style.stroke: "#4338ca"; style.bold: true
}

server: "Relay\ncairnd — ALWAYS ON" {
  style.fill: "#ecfdf5"
  t: "transports: HTTP/SSE (today), LAN" { style.font-size: 12 }
  r: "role: store-and-forward rendezvous\n+ allow-list, invites, directory" { style.font-size: 12 }
}
mobile: "Mobile (Wails3)\nembeds core" {
  style.fill: "#fefce8"
  t: "transports: HTTP, LAN, BLE, Meshtastic" { style.font-size: 12 }
  r: "full peer — signs, folds, syncs offline" { style.font-size: 12 }
}
browser: "Browser (PWA)\ncore → WASM" {
  style.fill: "#fff7ed"
  t: "transport: HTTP only" { style.font-size: 12 }
  r: "passkey ceremony stays in JS" { style.font-size: 12 }
}

core -> server: "compiled native" { style.stroke: "#4338ca" }
core -> mobile: "compiled native" { style.stroke: "#4338ca" }
core -> browser: "compiled to WASM" { style.stroke: "#4338ca"; style.stroke-dash: 3 }

server <-> mobile: "sync: LAN / HTTP" { style.stroke: "#047857" }
mobile <-> mobile2: "sync: BLE / mesh (no infra)" { style.stroke: "#047857" }
mobile2: "Mobile (field)\nembeds core + radio" { style.fill: "#fefce8" }
browser <-> server: "sync via a permanent node" { style.stroke: "#a16207"; style.stroke-dash: 3 }
server <-> server2: "relay-to-relay bridge\n(designed, not built)" { style.stroke: "#a16207"; style.stroke-dash: 3 }
server2: "Relay B\n(e.g. bridged over a radio link)" { style.fill: "#ecfdf5"; style.stroke-dash: 3 }

roadmap: |md
  **Built today:** the `transport.Transport` seam is **cut**, with HTTP/SSE registered
  as transport #1; one relay (cairnd) with a pinned keypair, allow-list, invites, and
  a directory; browser runs a parallel TS port. **Next:** LAN/BLE/Meshtastic behind
  the same interface, pubkey-addressed routing, symmetric node-to-node sync, and
  relay-to-relay bridging with interest-based routing. **Later:** the browser becomes
  a Go-WASM node (retiring the TS port). Browsers never do BLE/mesh — they reach the
  mesh *through* a permanent or mobile node.
| { style.font-size: 13; near: bottom-center }
